an update on a chicago woman who was  targeted by a botched police raid she is  speaking out for the very first time  since the city approved a 2.9 million  dollar settlement with her recently  a warning about this video of the raid  is extremely upsetting this body cam  footage from 2019 shows police officers  breaking down angenette young's door  while she was in the middle of changing  her clothes the officers left this  innocent woman naked and handcuffed for  a very long period of time she  repeatedly asked to get dressed and told  them they were at the wrong address  we spoke with young about the experience  yesterday but first let's take a look  back at what happened that night  this body cam footage from february 2019  shows a dozen male chicago police  officers storming into angenette young's  home on a thursday night  she was in the middle of changing her  clothes after returning from a work  event  young says the officers left her naked  and handcuffed for 40 minutes  they later learned that a police  informant gave them a bad tip  the person they were actually looking  for lived next door and was wearing an  electronic monitoring bracelet  city officials went to court to try to  block this video from airing after young  and her attorney shared it with our  chicago station wbbm  you got the wrong house this incident  happened before i was mayor  but is coming to light as i am mayor and  i have a responsibility to address this  issue chicago mayor lori lightfoot  initially denied knowing what happened  to young but later admitted she had been  briefed on the incident in november 2019  a third-party investigation ordered by  lightfoot found failures in oversight  and accountability by multiple city  agencies but determined that the mayor  and the city did not purposefully  conceal information about the botched  raid  now a newly released summary of a  scathing report by chicago's inspector  general finds the city did work to  prevent young from obtaining body cam  video and that it prioritized  communications and public relations  concerns over the higher mission of city  government  mayoral lightwood says she does not  agree with that conclusion  an oversight agency has recommended that  eight of the officers involved in the  incident be suspended or fired so far  none of them have  and angenette young now joins us in the  studio it's good to see you  thank you for having me you know i  remember antoinette when i first saw  that video it made me so angry and it  made me so sad because i could hear the  fear and i could hear the pain in your  voice as you look at it again what do  you remember about that night  um watching that video is always hard  because it just takes me back to that  moment  and one thing that stands out the most  to me as i think about this and lived  through this over the years is how  vulnerable i was in that moment what  were you doing at the time when the  police officers broke through the door  what were you doing i was getting  undressed  in the fact that i continued to ask if i  could get dressed i continued to ask  them if i could call someone  what stands out to me most is that i was  invisible to them  um because no one even responded to me  saying yes you can get dressed later or  let us finish this like they just  totally ignored me then while you're  standing there news why i'm standing  there nude and pleading yes  how has this experience affected you now  so i tell people that  i didn't lose my life that night  but i lost a lot of my life that night  at some point did you think you could  lose your life  i actually thought that if i had done  anything different than what i was doing  i would have died that night they yell  put your hands up and that's what i did  and i stood there in fear  and praying and hoping that they would  not shoot me last month the city  approved a two point new 2.9 million  dollar settlement for you  is that justice to you  because i could see some people looking  at this and saying well at least she got  paid  um the money is not justice and i never  went into this when i started the legal  um proceedings i never went in looking  for money what were you looking for when  i hired my attorney i immediately told  him that i wanted them all fired and i  say that because in any other profession  if you go to work and you harm someone  in this type of way you lose your job  as a social worker if i go to work and i  harm a client i will lose my job i will  lose my license just last week the  chicago office of inspector general  released a summary of the report and the  summary says that the city did in fact  mishandle your case across the board  so it's validating for me that over the  last three years i've been saying that  i've been saying that this is not right  but it doesn't  add to justice because  they need to make changes in policy  they need to make cultural changes in  the way that the police department  operates and they need to get rid of  some old boys that are in there  training new people to operate in this  way you say cultural changes do you  think the fact that you are a person  that you are a black woman do you think  that played a role in this i absolutely  think it played a role why so not so  much that i'm a black woman but that my  skin color is different from all of the  men who were in that room that night and  that validate to me that they had no  concern or passion for people and  families of color  and they've done this to other families  primarily in the black and brown  communities what happened to me does not  happen in the white  communities in chicago you know and  listen the only reason why we were even  seeing this tape is because you had to  go to court to get the tape yes you  fought to have this tape released yes do  you regret that so many of us have seen  you at your most vulnerable time i do  not don't it was a very hard decision to  agree to allow this to be played yes  but it was very necessary in the sense  that at the point that we  actually were able to get the body cam  footage after many attempts of not being  able to get it it was necessary because  at that point for me it was about i need  to expose what's happening here you know  the mayor hired a private firm to  investigate the case and they came to  the conclusion that the mayor's office  didn't have malicious intent in the  response to the case and they did not  deliberately conceal any information  what are your thoughts about that they  did a public showing of their results in  the entire time  i was in tears watching them stand  before the public and say that they did  nothing wrong  when everyone knows that they hid the  information initially  and then went to court to try to bend  cbs 2 chicago from airing it  the mayor lied continuously that entire  week when the video came out only later  to retract her information so how do you  not call that  intent  when do you think you will finally feel  at peace  i don't ever think that this will go  away i'm choosing to find ways to live  on purpose and not allow this one  incident to define me for the rest of my  life  has the money changed your life  no no no um  the money is almost an insult  in a sense that is saying  okay this is what we value that  situation to be and so  here's some money to help you with that  and even the mayor said you know this  amount of money will allow her to have  resources to take care of herself  there's no amount of money that  will right this wrong  i'm not a very flashy person i really do  enjoy living a simple life where i go  about my day as a social worker and and  i find joy in helping other people so i  would definitely be using this money for  that  cause to help other people  i would have  been  more satisfied  if all 12 officers had got fired and i  didn't receive a dime  wow yeah it's interesting you would  think  the money would bring her some comfort  and she says she wants to use it to help  other people she's a social worker by  training tony and i we both notice the  same thing on the wall where it says  faith hope and love she really is that  type of person she says she now suffers  from ptsd she's easily triggered by loud  things i'm happy to say she no longer  lives in that apartment  but she has not been able to go back to  work because she says mentally she's not  ready and she goes to therapy every  wednesday every wednesday like clockwork  to help her get through this and that  was in 2019. i i was noticing the faith  faith hope love and the other  knickknacks there's a drawing from some  child in her life because it reminds  everybody out there that you may not  know her but you know this person yeah  she's a very familiar person in our  lives and you have to ask yourself what  led to this happening from the police  perspective and how can it never happen  again like these sorts of mistakes need  to be prevented at the root we need to  find that root for a lot of people their  worst fear is being naked in front of  somebody exactly and she was exactly she  stood there nude for 40 minutes yes  ignored her as she was exposed there is  this poetic justice in her strength  saying release the tape yeah at least if  i want people to see me yeah she said  she thought about it nate and now she  has the police in chicago exposed just  like she was you know the thing is  nobody from the police department has  ever apologized to her wow

